#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "952dcbbf-8f32-59bc-9b71-0127fd4350f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(w+3)^{2}(w-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275738} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ea36a-85f1-5856-aa4d-8932e18d6b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275739} {:answer/id #uuid "285a426e-8f91-5582-863f-971c34aa0b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275740} {:answer/id #uuid "02ceb753-6bd5-53ab-92d9-dc40b24dce1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(w+3\\text{i})^{2}(w-3\\text{i})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275741} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96c354-1ce6-545a-a75d-8e3d18ede5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}w+3)^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ea36a-85f1-5856-aa4d-8932e18d6b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275739} :answer-field/id #uuid "554ecc00-8e54-5464-bf44-c883a620928f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d10758e2-afad-59fb-a650-755ee75719ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-170502" :question/problem "Factor $4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${w}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81 & ={(2{w}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (2{w}^{2})\\cdot 9+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{w}^{2}+9)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+9 & =2{w}^{2}-(-9) \\\\  & =2{w}^{2}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w)^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81 & =(2{w}^{2}+9)^{2} \\\\  & =((\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i}))^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dbf3ef3-1509-5a91-80c6-2d11e6eac885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12(t+3i)(t-3i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dbf3ef3-1509-5a91-80c6-2d11e6eac885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12(t+3i)(t-3i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275745} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a9d2cef-71b1-5ced-a483-cc981266043a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f99c5aa8-3278-5c9d-822e-715dedb6f9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-320782" :question/problem "Write $-12{t}^{2}-108$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-12{t}^{2}-108=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out $-3$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n-12{t}^{2}-108=-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(4{t}^{2}+36).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}+36 & =4{t}^{2}-(-36) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2t)^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36)=-3(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c55fcfa4-0050-5a80-a142-29616155797f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Quartic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c102f60-4f42-52a2-a654-cc1d89d2600c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y+2i)^2(3y-2i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c102f60-4f42-52a2-a654-cc1d89d2600c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3y+2i)^2(3y-2i)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275748} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4fe303d-c8f6-54f5-bd8d-1dda2971e215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58e69aa8-4b56-56cf-befa-b88244dc717a" :question/math-academy-id "q-367716" :question/problem "Write $81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${y}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16 & ={(9{y}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (9{y}^{2})\\cdot 4+{4}^{2} \\\\  & ={(9{y}^{2}+4)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+4 & =9{y}^{2}-(-4) \\\\  & =9{y}^{2}-4({i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(3y)}^{2}-{(2i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3y+2i)(3y-2i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16 & ={(9{y}^{2}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((3y+2i)(3y-2i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3y+2i)}^{2}{(3y-2i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2de6dbad-0ca5-5a17-a04b-cdfd72d6dab5" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring an Expression Containing a Sum of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba9b9ba-4511-5e1d-85fb-1a714da4e619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5(3+4\\text{i}m)(3-4\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275751} {:answer/id #uuid "48139f2d-45af-5845-aa6a-640a9edeeb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275752} {:answer/id #uuid "a1962e96-7b56-5ef8-a3a7-a8287d02e2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3+5\\text{i}m)(3-5\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275753} {:answer/id #uuid "0af1d39f-0431-5901-8bbc-118b75f49f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275754} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5b85b-46b7-5344-8cf1-54060d7c191c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5(4+3\\text{i}m)(4-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0af1d39f-0431-5901-8bbc-118b75f49f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275754} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b8e327-1845-5866-af7f-5a4c46fc9abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ce26373-4678-5527-bd99-a2df114e3db9" :question/math-academy-id "q-46507" :question/problem "The expression $-100-36{m}^{2}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor out $-4$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-100-36{m}^{2}=-4(25+9{m}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(25+9{m}^{2}).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25+9{m}^{2} & =25-(-9{m}^{2}) \\\\  & =25-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}{m}^{2}) \\\\  & ={5}^{2}-{(3\\text{i}m)}^{2} \\\\  & =(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into the expression, we get\n\n$$\n-4(25+9{m}^{2})=-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8216c637-fe4d-51fd-b256-0379eb0e7dba" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Sum of Higher-Degree Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd8d880e-0d86-55ad-8530-390642a4d6cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275758} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d90059-ca49-52bf-9079-83f438e442c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{5}+8\\text{i})({y}^{5}-8\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275759} {:answer/id #uuid "7e63009a-9f87-5cb7-be33-85e8ed3c928a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({y}^{5}-8\\text{i})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275760} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0f5af8-950f-5aeb-b8ad-e2770620265e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{10}+4)({y}^{10}-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275761} {:answer/id #uuid "25bbbab9-548d-50ab-84be-427fd22ce090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd8d880e-0d86-55ad-8530-390642a4d6cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275758} :answer-field/id #uuid "623d3b99-3299-502b-867f-a07cdcf72a2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b16b57c3-ee59-586a-bb9f-9545df8aaaa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-87322" :question/problem "Factor ${y}^{10}+16.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{y}^{10}+16={({y}^{5})}^{2}+{4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{10}+16 & ={y}^{10}-(-16) \\\\  & ={y}^{10}-(16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({y}^{5})}^{2}-{(4\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
